WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.069
-Our next guest is a very
funny comedian and actor

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.570
you know from "The Daily Show"
and movies

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:05.972
like "Crazy Rich Asians"
and "M3GAN."

00:00:05.972 --> 00:00:08.440
He's currently performing
stand-up across the country

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.209
on the
"Love to Hate It Tour,"

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:12.345
including this Saturday
at Radio City Music Hall.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:14.681
Please welcome to the show,
Ronny Chieng, everybody.

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:16.983
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:30.596 --> 00:00:32.598
Welcome. How are you?
-Thank you!

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:33.766
Thanks for having me!

00:00:33.766 --> 00:00:34.834
-I'm so thrilled
to have you here.

00:00:34.834 --> 00:00:37.770
-What an institution
to be here, man.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.640
I've been watching you forever.
-Well, I really appreciate that.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.441
I've been a big fan
of everything you're doing.

00:00:42.441 --> 00:00:44.777
And it's a very exciting
new chapter at "The Daily Show"

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:45.978
that's starting.

00:00:45.978 --> 00:00:47.646
Jon Stewart is coming back
to host on Monday.

00:00:47.646 --> 00:00:50.049
-Yeah, yeah. Jon Stewart's back.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:51.918
-And then the correspondents
are going to host

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.352
from Tuesdays to Thursdays.

00:00:53.352 --> 00:00:55.855
This has to be really exciting.
-Yeah. It's great.

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:59.258
Who better to take over the show

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:00.926
than the guy
who invented the show?

00:01:00.926 --> 00:01:02.261
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:04.596
Yeah, the guy who pioneered
modern American satire.

00:01:04.596 --> 00:01:06.032
So, he's back. He's back.

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:07.700
-As choices go,
it's a pretty easy one to make.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.336
-Yeah, it only took a more
than a calendar year

00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:12.204
to make that choice,
so there you go.

00:01:12.204 --> 00:01:14.106
-It does -- It is funny
that it took a calendar year

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:15.708
to come back to the last guy
who had the job.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:18.944
-Yeah, I know.
So, great decision-making.

00:01:18.944 --> 00:01:21.247
-Certainly, it was
in great hands in the years

00:01:21.247 --> 00:01:23.182
in between, with Trevor Noah
and the rest of you.

00:01:23.182 --> 00:01:24.851
Congratulations.
You won the Emmy last week.

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:27.019
-Yes, we won the Emmy
with Trevor Noah.

00:01:27.019 --> 00:01:29.555
He's my guy, Trevor. Noah.
-There you go.

00:01:29.555 --> 00:01:32.124
-He's the reason I was able
to come to America.

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:34.327
I love Trevor. Emmy deserved.

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:39.665
And, you know,
it's such a -- It's --

00:01:39.665 --> 00:01:41.500
You know,
I grew up in Malaysia,

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.869
and I'm such a fan
of American institutions,

00:01:43.869 --> 00:01:44.971
just like yourself.

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:46.139
And who knew?

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:49.908
Who knew that one day,
I could come to America,

00:01:49.908 --> 00:01:54.647
come to New York City, do
comedy, join "The Daily Show,"

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:58.551
be in the same Emmy category
as you and defeat you...

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:01.220
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.555
[ Cheers and applause ]
-We're the best show.

00:02:03.555 --> 00:02:04.724
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-This show.

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:08.361
-That proves it.
-We won. We're the champions.

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:12.699
Love it or hate it, we won.
We're the better show.

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:16.035
We're objectively
the better late-night show.

00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:17.837
Yeah. Yeah.
Boo. Boo all you want.

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:21.941
-I'm gonna take this away.
-No, we got to keep this going.

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:23.909
We got to start
beef between the shows.

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:26.179
That's how -- Come on.
Say some [bleep]

00:02:26.179 --> 00:02:27.880
-I don't want beef with you.

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.382
-This is how we
save our networks.

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:31.851
We got to talk [bleep]
about each other's show.

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:33.152
I'm trying to start
late-night war.

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:36.956
Let's go. Late-night wars!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:38.590
This is why I moved
to America for --

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.793
for late-night-talk-show drama.

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:44.264
We have a strong history of
late-night-talk-show drama

00:02:44.264 --> 00:02:46.832
in this country,
all the way from Carson

00:02:46.832 --> 00:02:48.401
to Letterman to Conan and Leno.

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:49.536
We gotta keep this going,
alright?

00:02:49.536 --> 00:02:51.137
-Yeah, there's a
lack of beef right now.

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:52.171
-Yeah. I'll start.
-Alright, you start. Go.

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:53.306
-I'll start the beef, okay?

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:55.541
I'm going to say
some [bleep] about this.

00:02:55.541 --> 00:03:00.346
"Late Night with Seth Meyers" --
this show is on too late.

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:02.648
-Yeah, you're right.

00:03:02.648 --> 00:03:06.685
-Okay? If you're watching
this live, go to bed!

00:03:06.685 --> 00:03:08.054
Go to bed.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:09.822
-Okay, your turn, your turn.
Go, go.

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:12.658
-This show is on too late.

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:14.360
-Yeah, that's why we won the --
Oh, wait.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.463
-I want to say, because you have
gotten in trouble for your --

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:19.866
Sometimes, you speak your mind,
like you just did.

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:21.034
-Sure.

00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:22.869
-You hosted
the International Emmy Awards.

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:24.336
I'm going to tell you a secret.
I did this once, too.

00:03:24.336 --> 00:03:25.471
-Oh, yeah, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:26.772
It's just a hotel
down the street.

00:03:26.772 --> 00:03:29.842
It's literally
a hotel in Midtown.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:31.511
-Yes, yes.
-The International Emmy Awards.

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:35.248
And it's a really wacky gig.

00:03:35.248 --> 00:03:37.417
-It is, and we are
in the alumni of people

00:03:37.417 --> 00:03:38.952
who have hosted
the International Emmys.

00:03:38.952 --> 00:03:41.386
John Oliver also hosted it.

00:03:41.386 --> 00:03:43.522
Before I took the gig,
I asked him if I should do it.

00:03:43.522 --> 00:03:45.325
He said, "Go ahead."

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:50.563
Be clear --
International Emmys, big deal

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:54.200
for -- as someone who
doesn't come from America,

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.501
who respects American
show business.

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:56.702
-Of course.

00:03:56.702 --> 00:03:58.337
-Big deal for
all the participants involved.

00:03:58.337 --> 00:04:00.239
-And great shows, I will say.
-Great shows.

00:04:00.239 --> 00:04:02.241
I will probably not get
to host it again, though.

00:04:02.241 --> 00:04:04.309
-Okay.
Thank you for your honesty.

00:04:04.309 --> 00:04:06.545
-Because -- No, I don't think
they'll have me back,

00:04:06.545 --> 00:04:09.748
because when I hosted it,
the one time I hosted it,

00:04:09.748 --> 00:04:12.918
we had, like,
a "La La Land" incident.

00:04:12.918 --> 00:04:18.991
So, I'm hosting the show.
I come out. I do a bit.

00:04:18.991 --> 00:04:22.695
Part of the introduction is,
I introduce the accountants

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:24.530
from the top-four --
one of the top-four

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.833
accounting firms in the --
I can't remember which one.

00:04:26.833 --> 00:04:28.133
-As long as it was top four,
I don't care.

00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:29.968
-Right.
It's one of the top four.

00:04:29.968 --> 00:04:32.137
And I'm like, "Oh,
these guys are in charge of

00:04:32.137 --> 00:04:33.906
auditing the votes,

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:35.441
making sure
the winners are legit.

00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:37.409
Give it up
for these accountants."

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:39.379
And everyone's like,
"Yeah, accountants."

00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:41.748
And then I introduced
John Turturro

00:04:41.748 --> 00:04:43.082
to present the first award.

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:44.250
So then John Turturro comes out.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.052
He's supposed
to present the award

00:04:46.052 --> 00:04:47.953
for -- I can't remember what.

00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:50.722
Like, it was TV Movie.

00:04:50.722 --> 00:04:53.459
And he opens the --
He reads out the nominees,

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:55.294
he opens the winner envelope,

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:59.765
and he reads out a name
that is not any of the nominees.

00:04:59.765 --> 00:05:04.270
And it turns out he announced
the winner for the Best Drama,

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:05.938
which is the last award, yeah.

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:09.008
So he ruined the whole suspense
of the whole thing.

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:10.075
And, you know,
it's not his fault.

00:05:10.075 --> 00:05:11.144
He was just reading.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.845
And he goes,
"I just read the thing.

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:14.346
I didn't..."

00:05:14.346 --> 00:05:17.449
And then he goes offstage, and
Lou Diamond Phillips was there.

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:18.584
It was a thing.

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:22.355
And then I come out
after that and I go,

00:05:22.355 --> 00:05:23.722
"These [bleep] accountants.

00:05:23.722 --> 00:05:26.425
You guys had one --

00:05:26.425 --> 00:05:31.898
You had job
and you messed it up."

00:05:31.898 --> 00:05:36.269
And no one was happy about that,
so I don't think --

00:05:36.269 --> 00:05:38.504
I don't think I'll get a chance
to host the International Emmys

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:41.340
again, but...

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.543
-One of the things I've been
very excited to meet you --

00:05:43.543 --> 00:05:45.110
and tonight's the first night
we're meeting --

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.180
is you mentioned
you're from Malaysia,

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:48.848
and then you came
to the States as a kid

00:05:48.848 --> 00:05:50.349
and then Singapore
and then Australia.

00:05:50.349 --> 00:05:52.885
But in the States,
you were in my hometown.

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:54.453
You were in
Manchester, New Hampshire.

00:05:54.453 --> 00:05:55.787
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:55.787 --> 00:05:58.023
-And you were a kid --
younger, obviously, than I am.

00:05:58.023 --> 00:06:01.927
But what was your take on
"ManchVegas" back in the day?

00:06:01.927 --> 00:06:04.030
-Yeah, Manchester,
New Hampshire -- it was --

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.732
So, my parents went to college
very late in life.

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:10.102
They had two kids
and then they went to college --

00:06:10.102 --> 00:06:11.470
in Malaysia.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:15.007
And then they went to college
and they put themselves

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:18.444
through college in Manchester,
New Hampshire, with two kids,

00:06:18.444 --> 00:06:19.812
and they got their master's.

00:06:19.812 --> 00:06:22.581
And then we were good
immigrants, 'cause after we got

00:06:22.581 --> 00:06:24.250
what we wanted, we went home.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:27.220
We didn't take anyone's job.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:28.954
We didn't --
You know, we were like,

00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:30.022
"Okay, we're going back."

00:06:30.022 --> 00:06:31.924
And then so
we went back after that.

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:33.459
And, so, I was away.

00:06:33.459 --> 00:06:35.594
I lived in Manchester,
New Hampshire, for like --

00:06:35.594 --> 00:06:38.197
was it 3 to 8?

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:39.632
So about four or five years.

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:41.166
And then I went back
to Malaysia.

00:06:41.166 --> 00:06:44.169
And when I was in Malaysia
and living in Singapore,

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:46.272
the whole time,
I'm like, "I need to go back.

00:06:46.272 --> 00:06:48.040
I need to go back
to Manchester, New Hampshire."

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.642
It's just in my head.

00:06:49.642 --> 00:06:52.011
It was this magical place
where there was snow

00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:53.845
and you could leave
your car unlocked.

00:06:53.845 --> 00:06:57.049
And there was like --
there was like one restaurant

00:06:57.049 --> 00:06:58.250
and, you know --

00:06:58.250 --> 00:06:59.986
And then I managed
to come back years later,

00:06:59.986 --> 00:07:01.920
after I joined "The Daily Show."

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:03.489
So it took me 25 years to
get back to

00:07:03.489 --> 00:07:04.890
Manchester, New Hampshire.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.092
And I was so psyched to --
I did a college gig

00:07:07.092 --> 00:07:08.760
at one of
the universities there.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:11.329
I went to go visit
my old elementary school,

00:07:11.329 --> 00:07:14.066
Webster Elementary,
and I went to go visit the park

00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:16.803
and the river and, like,
the three places I knew.

00:07:16.803 --> 00:07:18.270
-Yeah.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:19.772
-And I was like,
"Oh, this is actually

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:21.440
a very random place to be."

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.676
-Yeah.
-I had no idea when I was a kid.

00:07:24.676 --> 00:07:25.745
I thought this was
the whole world.

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:26.979
-Yeah.

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:28.313
-But now I go back as an adult,
and I'm like,

00:07:28.313 --> 00:07:29.481
"Why were we here?"

00:07:29.481 --> 00:07:33.552
-Yeah.
-There was nowhere else? Really?

00:07:33.552 --> 00:07:36.489
But -- Yeah, but I love
New Hampshire.

00:07:36.489 --> 00:07:37.823
-Yeah, no, that's that's
coming through.

00:07:37.823 --> 00:07:40.893
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:40.893 --> 00:07:42.662
-Granite State.
I love New Hampshire.

00:07:42.662 --> 00:07:45.264
-You were -- So, you actually
went to law school, correct?

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:46.933
-Yes. Yes, sir.
-Studied to be a lawyer.

00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:48.601
What kind of lawyer
do you think --

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:49.902
If you had not gone into comedy,

00:07:49.902 --> 00:07:51.703
what kind of lawyer do you think
you would have been?

00:07:51.703 --> 00:07:53.806
-I went to law school
in Australia

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:57.576
and I was, like,

00:07:57.576 --> 00:08:00.612
dabbling in, like,
international humanitarian law.

00:08:00.612 --> 00:08:02.814
I did, like, a summer program
at Oxford.

00:08:02.814 --> 00:08:04.851
And I think if I had graduated,

00:08:04.851 --> 00:08:07.620
I would have totally become
a douchebag corporate lawyer.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.721
-Yeah. That's good that you can
admit that about yourself.

00:08:09.721 --> 00:08:12.425
-Yeah. Because that's
what it was about.

00:08:12.425 --> 00:08:18.264
It was about -- It was about
the money and the power.

00:08:18.264 --> 00:08:19.698
But, so, I'm very lucky.
I came this close.

00:08:19.698 --> 00:08:21.099
I came this close.

00:08:21.099 --> 00:08:23.169
I could, like -- You know,
I was this close to becoming,

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:26.105
like, a Christian management
consultant in Singapore.

00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:29.041
-Yeah. And you just --
I mean, it must be a hard track

00:08:29.041 --> 00:08:30.676
to jump off and land in comedy.

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:32.712
Like, that is a very
dicey maneuver.

00:08:32.712 --> 00:08:33.979
It's incredible
how well it's gone.

00:08:33.979 --> 00:08:35.280
-I know.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.215
I'd like to thank the
five Christian Singaporean girls

00:08:37.215 --> 00:08:40.085
who rejected me,
and then...

00:08:40.085 --> 00:08:43.622
If even one of them
had accepted me as a boyfriend,

00:08:43.622 --> 00:08:45.758
I would be in Singapore
as an accountant right now

00:08:45.758 --> 00:08:47.026
or something, so thank you.

00:08:47.026 --> 00:08:48.594
Thank you to all the women.

00:08:48.594 --> 00:08:50.462
-This is a very cool photo
from 2009.

00:08:50.462 --> 00:08:53.266
This you and your
then-girlfriend, now wife?

00:08:53.266 --> 00:08:54.399
-Yes.
-Who's lovely.

00:08:54.399 --> 00:08:55.400
I just met her backstage.

00:08:55.400 --> 00:08:56.802
This is you guys
in Times Square.

00:08:56.802 --> 00:08:59.372
First time in Times Square?
-First time in Times Square.

00:08:59.372 --> 00:09:03.008
This is 2009,
the blurry potato photos,

00:09:03.008 --> 00:09:06.345
'cause that's what cameras
were like in 2009, kids.

00:09:06.345 --> 00:09:08.413
This was as good as it got,
the resolution.

00:09:08.413 --> 00:09:10.148
-This is not user error.

00:09:10.148 --> 00:09:11.551
That's just how
good cameras were.

00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:13.418
-Yeah. We didn't
mess up the dimensions.

00:09:13.418 --> 00:09:14.553
This is what it was.

00:09:14.553 --> 00:09:16.322
And, so, first time
in Times Square,

00:09:16.322 --> 00:09:18.324
and we took this photo here.

00:09:18.324 --> 00:09:20.960
And I just was in love
with New York

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:22.862
and I was like, "I need to come
back here and do comedy."

00:09:22.862 --> 00:09:24.163
-And not only did you
come back,

00:09:24.163 --> 00:09:25.698
work for "The Daily Show,"
but now you're on tour.

00:09:25.698 --> 00:09:26.866
You're going to play Radio City.

00:09:26.866 --> 00:09:29.568
And here's a photo
of you guys again.

00:09:29.568 --> 00:09:31.370
Ronny Chieng
poster in the background.

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:35.775
Better phone, better camera.
-Better camera. Yeah, yeah.

00:09:35.775 --> 00:09:37.610
And it's a dream come true,
so thanks, everyone,

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.611
who bought tickets
to the Radio City show.

00:09:39.611 --> 00:09:40.979
It's amazing.
-Yeah. Fantastic.

00:09:40.979 --> 00:09:42.214
Congratulations on Radio City.

00:09:42.214 --> 00:09:43.215
Congratulations on
"The Daily Show."

00:09:43.215 --> 00:09:44.516
-Thank you.
Thank you for having me.

00:09:44.516 --> 00:09:47.786
-What a pleasure to meet you.
Ronny Chieng, everybody.

00:09:47.786 --> 00:09:49.922
For information on his tour,
visit ronnychieng.com.

00:09:49.922 --> 00:09:51.390
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

